<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Íñigo Arista</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 790–<a href="/years/851">851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Eneko Arista, Íñigo Íñiguez</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Íñigo Arista was the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, the kingdom later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. A Basque leader, he took power around 824 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> defeated a Frankish army at the second Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Roncevaux</a>, ending Carolingian control. He was allied by kinship with the Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, since his mother had married into the family, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-musa">Musa ibn Musa</a> his half-brother. His dynasty ruled Pamplona until 905.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inigo-arista.jpg" alt="Íñigo Arista" width="189" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I%C3%B1igo_Arista.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
